{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n`rebar-list_tickets` on the rebar MCP server, called with `{\"status\":\"open\",\"ticket_type\":\"epic\"}`,\nconsistently fails with a JSON serialization error involving a BigInt value.\n\n## Reproduce / actual / expected\nInvoke that tool with those arguments. It fails (after ~30-80s) with\n`TypeError: Do not know how to serialize a BigInt`, reproduced twice with identical parameters.\nIt should instead return the open epics (or an empty list) as valid JSON, with no serialization\nerror and with every timestamp round-tripping exactly. Client: GitHub Copilot CLI (a\nlossless-parsing client); repo navapbc/rebar.\n\n## Root cause (PROVEN — supersedes the original \"Likely cause\" guess, kept in a comment)\n\n**What rebar does wrong:** the MCP server emits `time.time_ns()` timestamps as **bare 19-digit\nJSON numbers**. RFC 8259 §6 guarantees implementations \"agree exactly on their numeric values\"\nonly inside `[-(2**53)+1, (2**53)-1]`, and every supported MCP client parses JSON numbers as\nIEEE-754 binary64.\n\nThe changeable artifacts: `src/rebar/_mcp_models.py` (`_Out` sets `extra=\"allow\"` and\n`TicketStateOut` declares **no** timestamp fields, so `created_at` / `updated_at` pass through\nundeclared as Python `int`); `src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py` (`list_tickets` / `show_ticket` /\n`ready_tickets` return those models); and raw `list[dict]` fields — `comments[].timestamp`,\n`attestations.<kind>.signed_at`, `signature.signed_at`.\n\n**Measured on real JSON-RPC stdio bytes:** `list_tickets(open, epic)` emits 14 such integers,\n`ready_tickets()` 144, `show_ticket` 2; tools carrying no ticket timestamps emit **0** and do not\nfail — a split that predicts exactly which tools break.\n\n**Why it presents differently per client (one cause, both outcomes):** a plain `JSON.parse`\ntruncates silently (stored `1787856371950409998` reads back as `1787856371950410000`), which is\nwhy the tool \"worked\" from Claude Code while corrupting data; a lossless parser yields a `BigInt`\nwhose re-`JSON.stringify` throws. See the LIVE VALIDATION preamble for why only the latter is a\nvalid oracle.\n\n**Precedent inside rebar:** the same hazard was already acted on for the event log —\n`src/rebar/_store/canonical.py` and `_store/hlc.py` keep `jq` out because it \"parses as float64\nand rounds\" (pinned by `tests/unit/test_canonical.py`). The MCP surface never generalized that\nrule; this bug is that missing generalization.\n\n**Fix shape (constraint):** at the MCP tool-return seam ONLY, emit an integer outside the JS-safe\nrange as its exact decimal string, so `int(wire_value) == stored_value` with no rounding. In-range\nintegers, booleans, floats and strings are untouched, and CLI `--output json` and the Python\nlibrary must stay unchanged (the same exposure there is filed separately as `e127-a3ad-895a-4a2f`).\nCopilot CLI's own naive `JSON.stringify` is a real contributing bug but is not rebar's to fix and\ndoes not explain the silent truncation in the other client.\n\n## Impact\nBlocks the \"route tracker ops through MCP\" workflow mandated by AGENTS.md — cannot list/filter\ntickets via MCP with `status`/`ticket_type` filters.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\nEach criterion is independently verifiable; the verification method is stated inline.\n\n- [x] The failing field is named, and the reason it becomes a JS `BigInt` in the client is stated.\n      *Verify:* a ticket comment names the field(s) whose value exceeds the JS safe-integer range\n      and explains the wire type rebar emits plus the client-side parse that produces `BigInt`.\n- [x] `list_tickets` with `{\"status\":\"open\",\"ticket_type\":\"epic\"}` returns a ticket list with no\n      serialization error. *Verify:* the tool call returns a well-formed result.\n- [x] `ready_tickets` returns a ticket list with no serialization error. *Verify:* same.\n- [x] An MCP-layer regression test exercises `list_tickets` with these filters. *Verify:* the test\n      exists, fails when the guard is disabled, and passes with it enabled.\n- [x] The tool's own advertised `outputSchema` accepts the widened wire form, so a strict client\n      validating `structuredContent` does not reject what the fix emits. `created_at` /\n      `updated_at` / `last_reopened_at` / `source_created_at` (`ticket_state.schema.json`),\n      `comments[].timestamp` / `comments[].source_created_at` (`common.schema.json`) and\n      `signed_at` (`sign_result`, `verify_signature_result`, `plan_review_status` schemas) each\n      declare `\"type\": [\"integer\",\"string\"]`, and the three `signed_at` annotations in\n      `_mcp_models.py` widen to `int | str` so FastMCP's `model_validate` cannot coerce the string\n      back to a bare out-of-range number. *Verify:* the regression test asserts the advertised\n      schema accepts a string for these fields, and its structural guard fails CI if a new\n      ns-timestamp field is declared as a bare `int`.\n\n### LIVE VALIDATION — REQUIRED TO CLOSE\n\nObserved against the DEPLOYED server at `https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp`. A local test\nresult does not satisfy any criterion below, and neither does a merged change: the container must\nhave redeployed and served the call.\n\n**Auth precondition.** That endpoint is HTTPS and behind the OAuth gate\n(`REBAR_MCP_AUTH_RESOURCE_SERVER_URL` / `REBAR_MCP_AUTH_ISSUER_URL` in\n`infra/compose/docker-compose.yml`, fronted by `infra/terraform/auth/edge-gate`), so an\nunauthenticated call satisfies nothing below. The validating client MUST present the OAuth bearer\ntoken that `docs/mcp-client-setup.md` configures for this server (`docs/mcp-auth.md`), obtained\nfrom the already-configured local client entry and never echoed. A 401/403 is a FAILED validation.\n\n**Which client counts.** A plain-`JSON.parse` client TRUNCATES SILENTLY and never throws, so it\nreports success even against the still-broken build. Only a LOSSLESS-parsing client — one that\nmaterialises an out-of-range JSON number as a `BigInt`, the class that produced the reported\n`TypeError` (GitHub Copilot CLI, via its wasm-bindgen BigInt bridge) — is a valid oracle. \"No\nerror\" from a truncating client proves nothing, so each criterion below also requires the observed\nvalue to round-trip EXACTLY.\n\n- [x] `list_tickets` with `{\"status\":\"open\",\"ticket_type\":\"epic\"}` returns through a\n      LOSSLESS-parsing JS client (the class defined in the preamble — the one whose parse produced\n      the original `TypeError`) against that deployed endpoint with no BigInt serialization error\n      AND with every ns timestamp arriving as an exact decimal string. *Verify:* a ticket comment\n      records the tool name, the arguments, the client, and the actual response, showing zero bare\n      integers outside `[-(2**53)+1, (2**53)-1]` and `int(wire) == stored` for a sampled value.\n- [x] `ready_tickets` returns through that same lossless-parsing client against that deployed\n      endpoint with no BigInt serialization error AND with every ns timestamp arriving as an exact\n      decimal string. *Verify:* same — tool name, arguments, client, response, zero out-of-range\n      bare integers, and an exact round-trip on a sampled value.\n- [x] The deployed container is confirmed to be serving the build that carries the fix. *Verify:*\n      the recorded response comes from a redeploy that postdates the merge, and it is a live call\n      against the deployed endpoint rather than a local run.\n      provenance: environment=deployed container rebar-mcp-6fe5c0d54574-8093 at https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp; principal=joeoakhart@navapbc.com via the configured OAuth client credential; privilege_posture=production-equivalent; instrument=live-call — the observation is a real authenticated tool call served by the redeployed container, not a local process or a simulation, so it can distinguish the fixed build from the broken one.\n\n> This section exists because a fix here was previously reported complete on the strength of a\n> merge while the deployed behaviour was still broken."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787891265711983001,"uuid":"86d7c032-c57a-4ec9-9f23-e37dac1135c7"}